<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-geostrophic-balance" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Geostrophic balance</title>

    <p>
        As shown in <xref ref="table-horizontal-equations-of-motion-scaled" text="type-global" />,
        the two largest terms in each of the horizontal equations of motion are the Coriolis acceleration term proportional to <m>\sin{\phi}</m>
        and the pressure gradient acceleration term,
        each of <m>\mathcal{O}(10^{-3})</m>.
        All other terms are at least one order of magnitude smaller
        (i.e., at least 10 times smaller).
    </p>

    <p>
        Neglecting all but these two terms in each equation produces the following simpler system:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-balance-x-start" number="yes">
                0 \amp = fv - \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-balance-y-start" number="yes">
                0 \amp = -fu - \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y} 
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-x-start" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-y-start" text="type-global" />
        represent a balance between the horizontal Coriolis force and the horizontal pressure gradient force.
        This balance is called <term>geostrophic balance</term>, <term>geostrophic equilibrium</term>, or <term>geostrophy</term>,
        and the corresponding horizontal wind produced by this balance is called the <term>geostrophic wind</term>.
        The geostrophic wind is given by 
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-basic" number="yes">
            \vec{v}_g = u_g \, \hat{i} + v_g \, \hat{j}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        so we can write <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-x-start" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-y-start" text="type-global" />
        as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-balance-x" number="yes">
                0 \amp = f v_g - \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-balance-y" number="yes">
                0 \amp = -f u_g - \frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y} 
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        respectively,
        each of which can be rearranged to give the geostrophic wind components
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-y" number="yes">
                v_g \amp = \frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x" number="yes">
                u_g \amp = -\frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-y" text="type-global" /> and <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x" text="type-global" />
        show that the meridional (zonal) component of the geostrophic wind
        depends on the zonal (meridional) component of the horizontal pressure gradient.
        This dependency can be written compactly as in vector form as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector" number="yes">
            \vec{v}_g = \frac{1}{\rho f} \, \hat{k} \times \nabla p
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Given this definition,
        the geostrophic wind is a horizontal wind that flows locally perpendicular to the pressure gradient and,
        consequently,
        locally parallel to surrounding isobars.
        Since the acceleration terms <m>\dfrac{Du}{Dt}</m> and <m>\dfrac{Dv}{Dt}</m> are neglected in geostrophic balance,
        the geostrophic wind is non-accelerating and,
        therefore,
        flows in a straight line at constant speed.
        This produces a <term>diagnostic</term> (descriptive) relationship for the wind,
        not a <term>prognostic</term> (predictive) relationship.
    </p>

    <p>
        And the geostrophic wind is only valid for inviscid flow along a flat surface with no vertical motion.
    </p>

    <p>
        Clearly,
        the above constraints do not apply perfectly to motions in Earth's atmosphere:
        Real winds accelerate by changing speed and direction and have vertical components;
        air is viscous;
        and winds flow along and parallel to Earth's curved surface.
        Furthermore,
        <m>\vec{v}_g</m>'s inverse proportionality to the Coriolis parameter
        means that the geostrophic wind cannot apply at the Equator, where <m>f = 0</m>,
        and the geostrophic wind magnitude greatly overestimates the magnitude of the real wind at tropical latitudes where <m>f</m> is very small.
    </p>

    <p>
        But as <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>
        and <xref ref="holton-hakim" text="custom">Holton and Hakim (2013)</xref> point out,
        observed mid-latitude synoptic scale horizontal motions typically are within 10<ndash />15% of the geostrophic wind.
        Thus,
        geostrophic balance is a useful concept to apply to mid-latitude synoptic scale horizontal motions,
        and it is worth determining how close to geostrophic real horizontal winds are
        so we know when and where to safely apply this approximation.
        As we will see in the next subsection,
        the Rossby number allows us to do this.
    </p>

</subsection>